---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import { headline, images } from "../data/dista";

const description =
  "Data pipelines in Mojo: checked by the compiler, distributed with Dista, recorded in Postgres so they resume and retry, documented for coding agents, and built on a native Iceberg stack published as tins on mojoshelf.";

const img = Object.fromEntries(images.rows.map((r) => [r.label, r]));
---

<Base title="Data processing in Mojo" description={description} bare={false}>
  <Hero />

  <section class="section section--alt" id="what">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <div class="home-points">
        <div class="home-point">
          <h2 class="home-point__title">Checked at compile time</h2>
          <p>
            An <a href="/s/orca">Orca</a> pipeline is a Mojo type. Its steps, their inputs and
            outputs, its partitions and its schedule are compile-time parameters, so the
            compiler checks every edge before anything runs, and each step declares the
            Iceberg tables it reads and writes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="home-point">
          <h2 class="home-point__title">Scalable and distributed</h2>
          <p>
            Steps hand their work to <a href="/s/dista">Dista</a>, which runs Mojo functions
            across a cluster as tasks and actors, keeping each result on the worker that made
            it. On AWS, <code>orca aws</code> gives each run workers of its own, scaling up
            from none while tasks wait.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="home-point">
          <h2 class="home-point__title">Reliable</h2>
          <p>
            Every run, attempt and log is recorded in Postgres. A failed step retries, a killed
            run resumes at the steps that didn't succeed, and a retried commit is recognised
            rather than written twice. Sensors start a pipeline when a table it reads changes,
            and lineage traces each snapshot back to the runs and code that made it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="home-point">
          <h2 class="home-point__title">Agent friendly</h2>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/skills" rel="noopener">magmalake's skills</a>
            teach a coding agent Orca, Dista, a whole magmalake app and fast Mojo data code.
            <a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a> has them, and the prompt from which
            an agent built a lake of GitHub activity that now runs on AWS.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="home-point">
          <h2 class="home-point__title">Fast, small, quick to build</h2>
          <p>
            On three nodes, eight queries over 1.4 billion taxi trips in S3 take
            {headline.dista.toFixed(1)} s a pass on Dista, against {headline.rust.toFixed(1)} s
            for Rust and {headline.ray.toFixed(1)} s for Ray. Dista's container image is
            {" "}{img.Dista.gzip} MB compressed, against {img.Rust.gzip} MB for Rust and
            {" "}{img.Ray.gzip} MB for Ray, and builds from cold in {img.Dista.build} s, against
            {" "}{img.Rust.build} s for Rust; <a href="/s/dista/performance#images">Dista's
            performance page</a> has the whole build. Underneath, Parquet, Iceberg and the codecs
            are Mojo <a href="/tins">tins</a>, measured against pyarrow on
            <a href="/performance">the performance page</a>, including where they lose.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .home-points {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .home-point__title {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .home-point p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
</style>
